1
00:00:00,503 --> 00:00:03,287
Welcome to AIB Life's service governance application.

2
00:00:03,417 --> 00:00:06,323
This walkthrough follows the complete journey, from operational extracts

3
00:00:06,333 --> 00:00:08,513
to a clear, evidence based view of performance.

4
00:00:09,545 --> 00:00:10,939
The flow is straightforward.

5
00:00:11,069 --> 00:00:14,789
Bring in the BaNCS extracts, apply the Schedule twenty three rules, investigate

6
00:00:14,799 --> 00:00:16,960
the results, and share a monthly governance pack.

7
00:00:17,068 --> 00:00:18,315
Let's follow that journey.

8
00:00:19,336 --> 00:00:21,974
The dashboard brings reporting periods into one place.

9
00:00:22,104 --> 00:00:25,028
At a glance, see which service levels missed target, how much work

10
00:00:25,038 --> 00:00:26,780
was measured, and what remains overdue.

11
00:00:27,777 --> 00:00:30,130
An operations analyst starts in Extracts.

12
00:00:30,251 --> 00:00:33,724
Upload the correspondence, cancellation, withdrawal, and workflow files

13
00:00:33,734 --> 00:00:35,110
as CSV or Excel.

14
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:38,191
The application identifies each extract from its contents.

15
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:41,748
The checklist confirms coverage of all five slots.

16
00:00:41,869 --> 00:00:45,068
File details show the records received, while the extract date and quality

17
00:00:45,078 --> 00:00:47,422
notes explain the evidence behind the reporting set.

18
00:00:48,443 --> 00:00:50,989
New files replace earlier files in the same slot.

19
00:00:51,101 --> 00:00:52,733
Every monthly pack is rebuilt.

20
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:56,374
Analysts can also rebuild on demand, remove files, or reload the delivered

21
00:00:56,384 --> 00:00:57,230
extract set.

22
00:00:58,244 --> 00:01:00,680
Next, choose a reporting month from the dashboard.

23
00:01:00,792 --> 00:01:03,091
Older periods are easy to find using search.

24
00:01:03,205 --> 00:01:06,875
Incomplete periods are clearly labelled, so a partial month stays in context.

25
00:01:07,911 --> 00:01:10,826
The SLA position compares five headline service levels with their

26
00:01:10,836 --> 00:01:11,563
targets.

27
00:01:11,674 --> 00:01:14,668
Met, missed, and overdue counts sit beside the verdict.

28
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:17,740
Unit linked performance expands into three component steps.

29
00:01:18,735 --> 00:01:22,423
Pass or fail uses completed items: met divided by met plus missed.

30
00:01:22,553 --> 00:01:26,017
The second rate also includes overdue open work, making unfinished service

31
00:01:26,027 --> 00:01:28,546
obligations visible alongside completed performance.

32
00:01:29,577 --> 00:01:32,151
Select a service level to open the underlying items.

33
00:01:32,262 --> 00:01:36,170
Each record shows its policy, product, handler, timing, and outcome.

34
00:01:36,303 --> 00:01:39,169
The reviewer can trace the result back to operational evidence.

35
00:01:40,193 --> 00:01:42,373
Outcome filters narrow the list immediately.

36
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:45,711
Here, the reviewer isolates missed items and compares the required deadline

37
00:01:45,721 --> 00:01:48,316
with the actual completion, using the same evidence that produced the

38
00:01:48,326 --> 00:01:49,117
monthly score.

39
00:01:50,128 --> 00:01:53,515
The Exceptions view brings failing service levels and failed items together.

40
00:01:53,627 --> 00:01:57,434
Switch between missed deadlines and open overdue work to focus the investigation.

41
00:01:57,548 --> 00:02:00,624
Even a passing service level can contain individual exceptions.

42
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:04,046
Data quality findings stay beside the results.

43
00:02:04,176 --> 00:02:07,658
They flag overdue items, unmatched approvals, exclusions, and incomplete

44
00:02:07,668 --> 00:02:10,519
periods, helping reviewers understand where the evidence needs attention

45
00:02:10,529 --> 00:02:11,786
before drawing conclusions.

46
00:02:12,795 --> 00:02:15,615
The governance lead opens Intelligence for the historical view.

47
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:18,534
This panel connects the latest complete month's position with patterns

48
00:02:18,544 --> 00:02:21,505
across the reporting window, including recurring misses and outstanding

49
00:02:21,515 --> 00:02:22,022
work.

50
00:02:23,027 --> 00:02:26,313
Executive insight turns the computed figures into a readable summary.

51
00:02:26,443 --> 00:02:30,108
It describes the latest position, the wider record, failure concentrations,

52
00:02:30,118 --> 00:02:31,192
and overdue backlog.

53
00:02:31,306 --> 00:02:34,519
The application also supports optional Bedrock generated wording.

54
00:02:35,536 --> 00:02:38,533
Monthly trends show how each service level compares with its target over

55
00:02:38,543 --> 00:02:39,041
time.

56
00:02:39,146 --> 00:02:42,070
Change the selected service level to explore another pattern, while the

57
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:45,019
service level record summarises complete months below target.

58
00:02:46,045 --> 00:02:49,024
The scope selector narrows the analysis to a chosen month and everything

59
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:49,715
before it.

60
00:02:49,845 --> 00:02:52,769
This filters the current reporting history, making the relevant window

61
00:02:52,779 --> 00:02:54,603
easier to discuss in a governance meeting.

62
00:02:55,619 --> 00:02:59,101
Failure concentrations highlight handlers, products, and transaction types

63
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:00,724
with unusually high failure rates.

64
00:03:00,854 --> 00:03:03,888
These patterns help direct investigation; they describe where the failures

65
00:03:03,898 --> 00:03:05,686
sit, rather than proving their cause.

66
00:03:06,711 --> 00:03:09,791
The backlog view groups open overdue items by service level and shows

67
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:10,793
the oldest deadline.

68
00:03:10,923 --> 00:03:14,222
This adds a practical view of unfinished work alongside the formal completed

69
00:03:14,232 --> 00:03:15,398
item performance rate.

70
00:03:16,402 --> 00:03:18,902
For a direct explanation, ask about the report.

71
00:03:19,032 --> 00:03:21,824
Here, we ask where non unit linked failures concentrate.

72
00:03:21,957 --> 00:03:25,165
The answer uses already computed report figures, keeping the discussion

73
00:03:25,175 --> 00:03:26,742
anchored to the available evidence.

74
00:03:27,744 --> 00:03:29,924
Finally, open the monthly governance pack.

75
00:03:30,054 --> 00:03:33,692
The summary, SLA position, exceptions, quality findings, and source

76
00:03:33,702 --> 00:03:36,604
evidence come together in a consistent document for the governance review.

77
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:40,450
Export as PDF opens the browser's print workflow.

78
00:03:40,578 --> 00:03:43,612
Save the pack as a PDF to share the monthly position, supported

79
00:03:43,622 --> 00:03:45,739
by the figures and evidence reviewed throughout the journey.

80
00:03:46,744 --> 00:03:49,409
From five extract slots to a traceable governance story.

81
00:03:49,513 --> 00:03:53,032
Prepare the evidence, review service performance, investigate exceptions,

82
00:03:53,042 --> 00:03:54,070
and share the result.

83
00:03:54,183 --> 00:03:56,664
AIB Life, with clarity at every step.
